4.147.m:
By you this vrata named Kāñcanapurī was performed in former times;
by that merit I, worshipped in the three worlds, was obtained as your husband."
Description of the Gift of a Maiden (Now, the Gift of a Maiden)

4.157.m:
Knowing all the rules, he who bestows the gift of the jewel-cow obtains a radiant celestial chariot;
freed from all defilement, accompanied by kinsmen, sons, and grandsons, resembling the God of Love in form, he indeed attains the abode of Śambhu.
Description of the Vow of the Gift of the Two-Faced Cow

4.158.m:
Yudhiṣṭhira said:
A cow in the process of giving birth is to be given to an eminent Brahmin;
by what rite, O knower of dharma, and what is the fruit of her gift?

The venerable Kṛṣṇa said:
A cow in the process of giving birth is obtained by exceedingly great merits, O best of kings;
certain men who possess an abundance of amassed merits obtain [her].

4.159.m:
Yudhiṣṭhira said:
And explain the ordinance of a thousand cows, O Janārdana;
at what time should it be given, and how should it be given?

Even a single cow possessed of many virtues, given properly to a virtuous recipient, purifies the donor's family for three generations;
as for the person who faithfully bestows a thousand cows here, O king, it is not possible for me to describe his reward.
Description of the Votive Observance of the Gift of a Bull

4.160.m:
Pure and purifying, the highest of all gifts,
a single draft-ox bearing a single yoke is equal to ten milch cows;
indeed, he alone is superior to the gift of ten milch cows.
